WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.042 --> 00:00:06.920
Chris Anderson: Shane, give us
a snapshot of you growing up

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:10.382
and what on Earth led you to get
interested in artificial intelligence?

00:00:10.423 --> 00:00:15.053
Shane Legg: Well, I got my first
home computer on my 10th birthday,

00:00:15.095 --> 00:00:16.805
and I --

00:00:16.846 --> 00:00:18.974
this was before the internet
and everything.

00:00:18.974 --> 00:00:21.518
So you couldn't just go
and surf the web and so on.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:24.104
You had to actually make stuff
yourself and program.

00:00:24.145 --> 00:00:25.730
And so I started programming,

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:29.192
and I discovered that in this computer
there was a world,

00:00:29.234 --> 00:00:30.652
I could create a world,

00:00:30.652 --> 00:00:33.405
I could create little agents
that would run around

00:00:33.446 --> 00:00:35.574
and chase each other
and do things and so on.

00:00:35.574 --> 00:00:38.159
And I could sort of,
bring this whole universe to life.

00:00:38.201 --> 00:00:42.539
And there was  sort of that spark
of creativity that really captivated me

00:00:42.539 --> 00:00:45.458
and sort of, I think
that was really the seeds

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:46.960
of my interest that later grew

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:49.337
into an interest
in artificial intelligence.

00:00:49.379 --> 00:00:53.174
CA: Because in your standard education,
you had some challenges there.

00:00:53.174 --> 00:00:56.761
SL: Yeah, I was dyslexic as a child.

00:00:56.803 --> 00:01:00.599
And so they were actually
going to hold me back a year

00:01:00.599 --> 00:01:02.017
when I was 10 years old,

00:01:02.058 --> 00:01:04.644
and they sent me off
to get my IQ tested to sort of,

00:01:04.686 --> 00:01:06.896
you know, assess how bad the problem was.

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:10.567
And they discovered I had
an exceptionally high IQ.

00:01:10.609 --> 00:01:13.737
And then they were a little bit confused
about what was going on.

00:01:13.737 --> 00:01:15.363
And fortunately, at that time,

00:01:15.405 --> 00:01:17.741
there was somebody in the town I lived in

00:01:17.782 --> 00:01:19.534
who knew how to test for dyslexia.

00:01:19.576 --> 00:01:22.954
And it turns out I wasn't actually
of limited intelligence.

00:01:22.996 --> 00:01:25.415
I was dyslexic, and that was the issue.

00:01:25.457 --> 00:01:27.667
CA: You had reason
from an early age to believe

00:01:27.709 --> 00:01:31.421
that our standard assumptions
about intelligence might be off a bit.

00:01:31.421 --> 00:01:35.425
SL: Well, I had reason, from an early age,
to sometimes doubt authority.

00:01:35.425 --> 00:01:36.426
(Laughter)

00:01:36.468 --> 00:01:39.554
You know, if the teacher thinks
you're dumb, maybe it's not true.

00:01:39.554 --> 00:01:41.723
Maybe there are other things going on.

00:01:41.765 --> 00:01:46.394
But I think it also created in me
an interest in intelligence

00:01:46.436 --> 00:01:49.230
when I sort of had
that experience as a child.

00:01:49.272 --> 00:01:50.732
CA: So you're credited by many

00:01:50.774 --> 00:01:54.361
as coining the term
“artificial general intelligence,” AGI.

00:01:54.402 --> 00:01:57.113
Tell us about 2001, how that happened.

00:01:57.113 --> 00:02:00.784
SL: Yeah, so I was approached
by someone called Ben Goertzel

00:02:00.784 --> 00:02:02.494
who I'd actually been working with,

00:02:02.494 --> 00:02:04.621
and he was going to write a book,

00:02:04.663 --> 00:02:08.166
and he was thinking
about a book on AI systems

00:02:08.166 --> 00:02:10.251
that would be much more
general and capable,

00:02:10.251 --> 00:02:12.712
rather than focusing
on very narrow things.

00:02:13.046 --> 00:02:15.256
And he was thinking
about a title for the book.

00:02:15.256 --> 00:02:18.551
So I suggested to him, "If you're
interested in very general systems,

00:02:18.551 --> 00:02:20.512
call it artificial general intelligence."

00:02:20.553 --> 00:02:21.763
And so he went with that.

00:02:21.763 --> 00:02:24.933
And then him and various other people
started using the term online

00:02:24.933 --> 00:02:26.142
and the internet,

00:02:26.142 --> 00:02:28.478
and then it sort of became
popularized from there.

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:31.231
We later discovered there was
someone called Mike Garrod,

00:02:31.231 --> 00:02:35.527
who published a paper in a security
nanotech journal in '97.

00:02:35.568 --> 00:02:38.279
So he is actually the first person
to have used the term.

00:02:38.279 --> 00:02:41.574
But it turns out he pretty much meant
the same thing as us anyway.

00:02:41.616 --> 00:02:44.035
CA: It was kind of an idea
whose time had come,

00:02:44.035 --> 00:02:46.246
to recognize the potential here.

00:02:46.246 --> 00:02:50.458
I mean, you made an early prediction
that many people thought was bonkers.

00:02:50.458 --> 00:02:51.626
What was that?

00:02:51.668 --> 00:02:54.379
SL: Well, in about 2001,

00:02:54.421 --> 00:02:59.259
a similar time to when I suggested
this term artificial general intelligence,

00:02:59.259 --> 00:03:02.929
I read a book by Ray Kurzweil, actually,
"Age of Spiritual Machines,"

00:03:02.971 --> 00:03:06.766
and I concluded that he was
fundamentally right,

00:03:06.808 --> 00:03:12.522
that computation was likely to grow
exponentially for at least a few decades,

00:03:12.564 --> 00:03:15.400
and the amount of data in the world
would grow exponentially

00:03:15.442 --> 00:03:16.609
for a few decades.

00:03:16.609 --> 00:03:19.279
And so I figured
that if that was going to happen,

00:03:19.279 --> 00:03:22.824
then the value of extremely
scalable algorithms

00:03:22.824 --> 00:03:26.119
that could harness
all this data and computation

00:03:26.161 --> 00:03:27.996
were going to be very high.

00:03:27.996 --> 00:03:31.458
And then I also figured
that in the mid 2020s,

00:03:31.499 --> 00:03:32.959
it would be possible then,

00:03:33.001 --> 00:03:35.712
if we had these highly
scalable algorithms,

00:03:35.712 --> 00:03:39.966
to train artificial intelligence systems

00:03:40.008 --> 00:03:43.428
on far more data than a human
would experience in a lifetime.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:44.888
And so as a result of that,

00:03:44.929 --> 00:03:48.767
you can find it on my blog
from about 2009

00:03:48.808 --> 00:03:51.394
I think it's the first time
I publicly talked about it,

00:03:51.436 --> 00:03:55.356
I predicted a 50 percent
chance of AGI by 2028.

00:03:55.815 --> 00:03:58.151
I still believe that today.

00:03:58.151 --> 00:03:59.819
CA: That's still your date.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:04.365
How did you define AGI back then,
and has your definition changed?

00:04:04.824 --> 00:04:08.995
SL: Yeah, I didn't have a particularly
precise definition at the beginning.

00:04:09.037 --> 00:04:13.625
It was really just an idea of systems
that would just be far more general.

00:04:13.666 --> 00:04:16.169
So rather than just playing
Go or chess or something,

00:04:16.211 --> 00:04:19.172
rather than actually be able to do many,
many different things.

00:04:19.214 --> 00:04:21.758
The definition I use now
is that it's a system

00:04:21.758 --> 00:04:24.385
that can do all the cognitive
kinds of tasks

00:04:24.385 --> 00:04:26.930
that people can do, possibly more,

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:29.349
but at least it can do
the sorts of cognitive tasks

00:04:29.349 --> 00:04:31.226
that people can typically do.

00:04:31.267 --> 00:04:34.270
CA: So talk about just
the founding of DeepMind

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:38.316
and the interplay between you
and your cofounders.

00:04:38.316 --> 00:04:42.320
SL: Right. So I went to London
to the place called the Gatsby Unit,

00:04:42.362 --> 00:04:47.408
which studies theoretical neuroscience
and machine learning.

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:49.828
And I was interested in learning
the relationships

00:04:49.828 --> 00:04:51.871
between what we understand about the brain

00:04:51.871 --> 00:04:53.748
and what we know from machine learning.

00:04:53.748 --> 00:04:55.708
So that seemed like a really good place.

00:04:55.708 --> 00:04:57.252
And I met Demis Hassabis there.

00:04:57.252 --> 00:04:59.254
He had the same postdoc supervisor as me,

00:04:59.295 --> 00:05:00.547
and we got talking.

00:05:00.588 --> 00:05:03.216
And he convinced me

00:05:03.258 --> 00:05:05.510
that it was the time
to start a company then.

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:08.179
That was in 2009 we started talking.

00:05:08.221 --> 00:05:09.889
And I was a little bit skeptical.

00:05:09.889 --> 00:05:13.434
I thought AGI was still
a bit too far away,

00:05:13.434 --> 00:05:16.855
but he thought the time was right,
so we decided to go for it.

00:05:16.896 --> 00:05:20.066
And then a friend of his
was Mustafa Suleyman.

00:05:20.733 --> 00:05:23.236
CA: And specifically,
one of the goals of the company

00:05:23.236 --> 00:05:25.071
was to find a pathway to AGI?

00:05:25.113 --> 00:05:26.447
SL: Absolutely.

00:05:26.447 --> 00:05:30.326
On our first business plan
that we were circulating

00:05:30.368 --> 00:05:33.413
when we were looking
for investors in 2010,

00:05:33.413 --> 00:05:35.999
it had one sentence
on the front cover and it said,

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:38.751
"Build the world's first artificial
general intelligence."

00:05:38.793 --> 00:05:41.462
So that was right in from the beginning.

00:05:42.046 --> 00:05:43.548
CA: Even though you knew

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:47.927
that building that AGI might actually have

00:05:47.969 --> 00:05:50.597
apocalyptic consequences
in some scenarios?

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:52.140
SL: Yeah.

00:05:52.181 --> 00:05:56.853
So it's a deeply
transformative technology.

00:05:57.437 --> 00:05:59.022
I believe it will happen.

00:05:59.856 --> 00:06:01.107
I think that, you know,

00:06:01.149 --> 00:06:04.903
these algorithms can be understood
and they will be understood at the time.

00:06:04.944 --> 00:06:07.906
And I think that intelligence
is fundamentally

00:06:07.906 --> 00:06:09.782
an incredibly valuable thing.

00:06:10.241 --> 00:06:13.077
Everything around us at the moment --
the building we’re in,

00:06:13.077 --> 00:06:16.748
the words I’m using, the concepts we have,
the technology around us --

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:20.627
you know, all of these things
are being affected by intelligence.

00:06:20.668 --> 00:06:23.546
So having intelligence in machines

00:06:23.588 --> 00:06:27.467
is an incredibly valuable
thing to develop.

00:06:27.467 --> 00:06:29.469
And so I believe it is coming.

00:06:29.510 --> 00:06:33.640
Now when a very, very
powerful technology arises,

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:36.267
there can be a range
of different outcomes.

00:06:36.309 --> 00:06:38.227
Things could go very, very well,

00:06:38.227 --> 00:06:40.813
but there is a possibility things
can go badly as well.

00:06:40.855 --> 00:06:45.818
And that was something I was aware of
also from about 20 years ago.

00:06:46.319 --> 00:06:49.572
CA: So talk about, as DeepMind developed,

00:06:49.572 --> 00:06:53.868
was there a moment where you really felt,

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:57.538
"My goodness, we're onto
something unbelievably powerful?"

00:06:57.580 --> 00:07:01.876
Like, was it AlphaGo, that whole story,
or what was the moment for you?

00:07:01.918 --> 00:07:04.420
SL: Yeah, there were many
moments over the years.

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:06.631
One was when we did the Atari games.

00:07:07.131 --> 00:07:08.383
Have you seen those videos

00:07:08.424 --> 00:07:11.844
where we had an algorithm
that could learn to play multiple games

00:07:11.844 --> 00:07:14.097
without being programmed
for any specific game?

00:07:14.138 --> 00:07:17.433
There were some exciting moments there.

00:07:17.433 --> 00:07:20.019
Go, of course, was a really
exciting moment.

00:07:20.937 --> 00:07:24.691
But I think the thing that's really
captured my imagination,

00:07:24.732 --> 00:07:26.192
a lot of people's imagination,

00:07:26.192 --> 00:07:29.445
is the phenomenal scaling
of language models in recent years.

00:07:29.821 --> 00:07:31.781
I think we can see they're systems

00:07:31.781 --> 00:07:35.451
that really can start
to do some meaningful fraction

00:07:35.493 --> 00:07:37.870
of the cognitive tasks that people can do.

00:07:37.912 --> 00:07:39.914
CA: Now, you were working on those models,

00:07:39.914 --> 00:07:41.874
but were you, to some extent, blindsided

00:07:41.874 --> 00:07:47.797
by OpenAI's, sort of,
sudden unveiling of ChatGPT?

00:07:47.839 --> 00:07:49.007
SL: Right.

00:07:49.007 --> 00:07:50.842
We were working on them and you know,

00:07:50.842 --> 00:07:52.969
the transformer model
was invented in Google,

00:07:53.011 --> 00:07:58.307
and we had teams who were building big
transformer language models and so on.

00:07:58.683 --> 00:08:01.853
CA: Google acquired DeepMind
at some point in this journey.

00:08:01.894 --> 00:08:03.229
SL: Yeah, exactly.

00:08:03.229 --> 00:08:07.275
And so what I didn't expect

00:08:07.275 --> 00:08:11.988
was just how good a model could get
training purely on text.

00:08:11.988 --> 00:08:14.699
I thought you would
need more multimodality.

00:08:14.741 --> 00:08:18.453
You'd need images, you'd need sound,
you'd need video and things like that.

00:08:18.494 --> 00:08:22.165
But due to the absolutely
vast quantities of text,

00:08:22.206 --> 00:08:24.959
it can sort of compensate
for these things to an extent.

00:08:25.376 --> 00:08:28.004
I still think you see aspects of this.

00:08:28.004 --> 00:08:31.174
I think language models
tend to be weak in areas

00:08:31.174 --> 00:08:34.385
that are not easily expressed in text.

00:08:34.385 --> 00:08:36.804
But I don’t think
this is a fundamental limitation.

00:08:36.804 --> 00:08:41.768
I think we're going to see these language
models expanding into video

00:08:41.809 --> 00:08:44.020
and images and sound and all these things,

00:08:44.020 --> 00:08:46.064
and these things will be overcome in time.

00:08:46.564 --> 00:08:47.899
CA: So talk to us, Shane,

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:50.902
about the things that you, at this moment,

00:08:50.902 --> 00:08:55.656
passionately feel that the world needs
to be thinking about more cogently.

00:08:55.698 --> 00:08:56.866
SL: Right.

00:08:56.866 --> 00:08:59.494
So I think that very, very powerful,

00:08:59.535 --> 00:09:03.039
very intelligent artificial
intelligence is coming.

00:09:03.498 --> 00:09:06.167
I think that this is very, very likely.

00:09:06.209 --> 00:09:07.794
I don't think it's coming today.

00:09:07.794 --> 00:09:10.338
I don't think it's coming next
year or the year after.

00:09:10.338 --> 00:09:12.757
It's probably a little bit
further out than that.

00:09:12.799 --> 00:09:14.092
CA: 2028?

00:09:14.092 --> 00:09:16.385
SL: 2028, that's a 50 percent chance.

00:09:16.385 --> 00:09:18.429
So, you know, if it doesn't
happen in 2028,

00:09:18.429 --> 00:09:20.431
I'm not going to be surprised, obviously.

00:09:20.473 --> 00:09:21.933
CA: And when you say powerful,

00:09:21.933 --> 00:09:24.060
I mean there's already
powerful AI out there.

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:25.853
But you're saying basically a version

00:09:25.853 --> 00:09:27.980
of artificial general
intelligence is coming.

00:09:28.022 --> 00:09:29.148
SL: Yeah.

00:09:29.190 --> 00:09:31.901
CA: So give us a picture
of what that could look like.

00:09:31.901 --> 00:09:34.570
SL: Well, if you had
an artificial general intelligence,

00:09:34.570 --> 00:09:36.989
you could do all sorts of amazing things.

00:09:37.031 --> 00:09:40.284
Just like human intelligence is able
to do many, many amazing things.

00:09:40.326 --> 00:09:42.328
So it's not really about a specific thing,

00:09:42.370 --> 00:09:44.330
that's the whole point of the generality.

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:46.082
But to give you one example,

00:09:46.082 --> 00:09:48.417
we developed the system AlphaFold,

00:09:48.459 --> 00:09:54.340
which will take a protein and compute,
basically, the shape of that protein.

00:09:54.382 --> 00:09:57.009
And that enables you to do
all sorts of research

00:09:57.009 --> 00:09:58.928
into understanding biological processes,

00:09:58.970 --> 00:10:01.556
developing medicines
and all kinds of things like that.

00:10:01.556 --> 00:10:03.015
Now, if you had an AGI system,

00:10:03.057 --> 00:10:05.643
instead of requiring
what we had at DeepMind,

00:10:05.685 --> 00:10:08.604
about roughly 30 world-class scientists

00:10:08.604 --> 00:10:11.274
working for about three years
to develop that,

00:10:11.315 --> 00:10:13.442
maybe you could develop that
with just a team

00:10:13.442 --> 00:10:15.653
of a handful of scientists in one year.

00:10:16.154 --> 00:10:19.907
So imagine these, sort of,
AlphaFold-level developments

00:10:19.949 --> 00:10:23.161
taking place around the world
on a regular basis.

00:10:23.202 --> 00:10:25.705
This is the sort of thing
that AGI could enable.

00:10:25.997 --> 00:10:30.042
CA: So within months of AGI
being with us, so to speak,

00:10:30.084 --> 00:10:33.171
it's quite possible that some
of the scientific challenges

00:10:33.171 --> 00:10:37.133
that humans have wrestled with
for decades, centuries, if you like,

00:10:37.133 --> 00:10:40.094
will start to tumble in rapid succession.

00:10:40.136 --> 00:10:44.265
SL: Yeah, I think it'll open up
all sorts of amazing possibilities.

00:10:44.265 --> 00:10:48.477
And it could be really
a golden age of humanity

00:10:48.519 --> 00:10:50.354
where human intelligence,

00:10:50.396 --> 00:10:54.775
which is aided and extended
with machine intelligence,

00:10:54.775 --> 00:10:57.236
enables us to do all sorts
of fantastic things

00:10:57.278 --> 00:11:02.200
and solve problems that previously
were just intractable.

00:11:02.200 --> 00:11:03.701
CA: So let's come back to that.

00:11:03.743 --> 00:11:05.328
But I think you also,

00:11:05.328 --> 00:11:07.580
you're not like,
an irredeemable optimist only,

00:11:07.622 --> 00:11:11.542
you see a potential for it to go
very badly in a different direction.

00:11:11.542 --> 00:11:14.253
Talk about what that pathway
could look like.

00:11:14.253 --> 00:11:16.214
SL: Well, yeah, I want to explain.

00:11:17.048 --> 00:11:18.633
I don't believe the people

00:11:18.674 --> 00:11:20.927
who are sure that it's going
to go very well,

00:11:20.968 --> 00:11:22.428
and I don't believe the people

00:11:22.428 --> 00:11:24.889
who are sure that it’s going
to go very, very badly.

00:11:24.889 --> 00:11:28.935
Because what we’re talking about
is an incredibly profound transition.

00:11:29.352 --> 00:11:32.563
It's like the arrival of human
intelligence in the world.

00:11:32.605 --> 00:11:35.566
This is another intelligence
arriving in the world.

00:11:35.608 --> 00:11:38.653
And so it is an incredibly
deep transition,

00:11:38.653 --> 00:11:41.781
and we do not fully understand
all the implications

00:11:41.781 --> 00:11:43.366
and consequences of this.

00:11:43.407 --> 00:11:44.825
And so we can't be certain

00:11:44.867 --> 00:11:47.328
that it's going to be this,
that or the other thing.

00:11:47.328 --> 00:11:50.539
So we have to be open-minded
about what may happen.

00:11:51.332 --> 00:11:53.251
I have some optimism because I think

00:11:53.251 --> 00:11:56.337
that if you want to make a system safe,

00:11:56.379 --> 00:11:59.048
you need to understand
a lot about that system.

00:11:59.090 --> 00:12:00.758
You can't make an airplane safe

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:03.427
if you don't know
about how airplanes work.

00:12:03.761 --> 00:12:06.013
So as we get closer to AGI,

00:12:06.055 --> 00:12:08.557
we will understand more
and more about these systems,

00:12:08.557 --> 00:12:11.560
and we will see more ways
to make these systems safe,

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:14.272
make highly ethical AI systems.

00:12:15.064 --> 00:12:18.651
But there are many things
we don't understand about the future.

00:12:18.693 --> 00:12:23.990
So I have to accept that there is
a possibility that things may go badly

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:26.075
because I don't know
what's going to happen.

00:12:26.117 --> 00:12:29.453
I can't know that about the future
in such a big change.

00:12:29.453 --> 00:12:34.292
And even if the probability of something
going bad is quite small,

00:12:34.333 --> 00:12:36.711
we should take this extremely seriously.

00:12:36.711 --> 00:12:39.588
CA: Paint a scenario
of what going bad could look like.

00:12:39.630 --> 00:12:41.340
SL: Well, it's hard to do

00:12:41.382 --> 00:12:43.134
because you're talking about systems

00:12:43.134 --> 00:12:46.637
that potentially have superhuman
intelligence, right?

00:12:46.637 --> 00:12:51.100
So there are many ways in which
things would go bad in the world.

00:12:51.142 --> 00:12:54.437
People sometimes point to, I don't know,
engineered pathogens, right?

00:12:54.437 --> 00:12:58.190
Maybe a superintelligence could
design an engineered pathogen.

00:12:58.190 --> 00:13:00.443
It could be much more mundane things.

00:13:00.484 --> 00:13:03.029
Maybe with AGI, you know,

00:13:03.070 --> 00:13:07.658
it gets used to destabilize
democracy in the world,

00:13:07.700 --> 00:13:10.828
with, you know, propaganda
or all sorts of other things like that.

00:13:10.870 --> 00:13:12.038
We don't know --

00:13:12.079 --> 00:13:14.081
CA: That one might already have happened.

00:13:14.081 --> 00:13:16.083
SL: There might be happening
a bit already.

00:13:16.125 --> 00:13:18.294
But, you know, there may
be a lot more of this

00:13:18.294 --> 00:13:19.920
if we have more powerful systems.

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:22.840
So there are many ways in which
societies can be destabilized.

00:13:22.882 --> 00:13:24.925
And you can see that in the history books.

00:13:24.967 --> 00:13:27.511
CA: I mean, Shane, if you
could have asked all humans,

00:13:27.511 --> 00:13:31.849
say, 15 years ago, OK,
we can open a door here,

00:13:31.891 --> 00:13:35.561
and opening this door could lead
to the best-ever outcomes for humanity.

00:13:35.603 --> 00:13:37.396
But there's also a meaningful chance,

00:13:37.396 --> 00:13:39.940
let's say it's more than five percent,

00:13:39.982 --> 00:13:42.735
that we could actually
destroy our civilization.

00:13:43.235 --> 00:13:46.572
I mean, isn't there a chance
that most people would have actually said,

00:13:46.614 --> 00:13:48.366
"Don't you dare open that damn door.

00:13:48.407 --> 00:13:49.575
Let's wait."

00:13:50.326 --> 00:13:54.413
SL: If I had a magic wand
and I could slow things down,

00:13:54.455 --> 00:13:56.791
I would use that magic wand, but I don't.

00:13:56.791 --> 00:13:58.626
There are dozens of companies,

00:13:58.626 --> 00:14:01.087
well, there's probably
10 companies in the world now

00:14:01.087 --> 00:14:05.508
that can develop the most
cutting-edge models, including, I think,

00:14:05.508 --> 00:14:09.595
some national intelligence services
who have secret projects doing this.

00:14:10.262 --> 00:14:11.764
And then there's, I don't know,

00:14:11.764 --> 00:14:15.267
dozens of companies that can develop
something that's a generation behind.

00:14:15.267 --> 00:14:18.646
And remember, intelligence
is incredibly valuable.

00:14:18.687 --> 00:14:19.855
It's incredibly useful.

00:14:19.855 --> 00:14:21.023
We're doing this

00:14:21.065 --> 00:14:24.151
because we can see all kinds of value
that can be created in this

00:14:24.151 --> 00:14:25.903
for all sorts of reasons.

00:14:25.903 --> 00:14:28.739
How do you stop this process?

00:14:28.781 --> 00:14:31.617
I don't see any realistic plan
that I've heard of,

00:14:31.659 --> 00:14:32.868
of stopping this process.

00:14:32.868 --> 00:14:34.078
Maybe we can --

00:14:34.078 --> 00:14:36.372
I think we should think
about regulating things.

00:14:36.414 --> 00:14:40.084
I think we should do things like this
as we do with every powerful technology.

00:14:40.084 --> 00:14:41.961
There's nothing special about AI here.

00:14:41.961 --> 00:14:45.423
People talk about, oh, you know,
how dare you talk about regulating this?

00:14:45.464 --> 00:14:49.301
No, we regulate powerful technologies
all the time in the interests of society.

00:14:49.343 --> 00:14:52.763
And I think this is a very important
thing that we should be looking at.

00:14:52.763 --> 00:14:56.517
CA: It's kind of the first time we have
this superpowerful technology out there

00:14:56.559 --> 00:14:59.645
that we literally don't understand
in full how it works.

00:14:59.645 --> 00:15:03.566
Is the most single, most important thing
we must do, in your view,

00:15:03.566 --> 00:15:08.237
to understand, to understand better
what on Earth is going on,

00:15:08.279 --> 00:15:11.449
so that we least have a shot
at pointing it in the right direction?

00:15:11.449 --> 00:15:14.743
SL: There is a lot of energy
behind capabilities at the moment

00:15:14.785 --> 00:15:17.830
because there's a lot of value
in developing the capabilities.

00:15:17.830 --> 00:15:22.334
I think we need to see a lot more energy
going into actual science,

00:15:22.376 --> 00:15:25.504
understanding how these networks work,

00:15:25.504 --> 00:15:27.673
what they're doing,
what they're not doing,

00:15:27.715 --> 00:15:29.550
where they're likely to fail,

00:15:29.592 --> 00:15:32.011
and understanding how we put
these things together

00:15:32.011 --> 00:15:34.096
so that we're able to find ways

00:15:34.138 --> 00:15:38.267
to make these AGI systems
profoundly ethical and safe.

00:15:38.267 --> 00:15:40.060
I believe it's possible.

00:15:40.895 --> 00:15:46.400
But we need to mobilize
more people's minds and brains

00:15:46.442 --> 00:15:48.944
to finding these solutions

00:15:48.986 --> 00:15:50.821
so that we end up in a future

00:15:50.863 --> 00:15:54.825
where we have incredibly
powerful machine intelligence

00:15:54.867 --> 00:15:57.703
that's also profoundly ethical and safe,

00:15:57.745 --> 00:16:00.498
and it enhances and extends humanity

00:16:00.539 --> 00:16:04.835
into, I think, a new
golden period for humanity.

00:16:05.920 --> 00:16:08.380
CA: Shane, thank you so much
for sharing that vision

00:16:08.380 --> 00:16:09.590
and coming to TED.

00:16:09.590 --> 00:16:11.050
(Applause)

